      "How much urea and DAP does one acre of wheat actually need? The reliable way is five steps: get a nutrient recommendation, check whether it is per hectare or per acre, convert the units, calculate each fertilizer with the standard formula, and split the dose across growth stages. Followed carefully, this turns any recommendation into an exact shopping list — for example, a 120-60-40 (illustrative) recommendation works out to about 1.7 bags of urea and 1 bag of DAP per acre.",
      "## Steps 1 to 3: recommendation and units",
      "Step 1 — Get a recommendation you trust. Everything starts with a nutrient target, written like 120-60-40 (kg of N–P₂O₅–K₂O). The best source is a soil test from your district soil laboratory — it tells you what your specific field needs. The next best is your provincial agriculture department's package of practices for the crop — Fertilizer Dose's calculator stores the verified, source-linked version of these recommendations for each crop and region, so check yours there. Never use a neighbour's dose as your recommendation — his soil is not yours. The worked example below uses the round figures 120-60-40 purely to show the arithmetic — they are illustrative, not a real recommendation for any crop. Always substitute your own verified figures.",
      "Step 2 — Check the unit: per hectare or per acre? Recommendations are usually published per hectare, but most Pakistani and Indian farmers think per acre. Mixing these up is the single most expensive mistake in fertilizer math: applying a per-hectare dose to every acre overdoses the field by about two and a half times. Before calculating anything, confirm which unit your recommendation uses.",
      "Step 3 — Convert to your unit. To go from per-hectare to per-acre, divide by 2.471. Our example: 120 ÷ 2.471 = 48.6 kg N per acre; 60 ÷ 2.471 = 24.3 kg P₂O₅ per acre; 40 ÷ 2.471 = 16.2 kg K₂O per acre. Other useful conversions: 1 acre = 4 kanal = 80 marla in Pakistan, so divide the per-acre figure by 4 for a kanal and by 80 for a marla; 1 hectare = 10,000 square metres; multiply acres by 0.4047 to get hectares. In India the acre is standard; the bigha varies by state, so always convert bigha to acres first using your state's definition.",
      "## Step 4: the dose formula",
      "Step 4 — Calculate each fertilizer with the standard formula: kg of fertilizer = (kg of nutrient needed ÷ % of that nutrient in the fertilizer) × 100. Working per acre with our converted figures (48.6 N, 24.3 P₂O₅, 16.2 K₂O): phosphate first with DAP (18-46-0): 24.3 ÷ 46 × 100 = 52.8 kg DAP per acre. Subtract DAP's free nitrogen: 52.8 × 0.18 = 9.5 kg N already supplied, leaving 48.6 − 9.5 = 39.1 kg N. Urea (46-0-0) for the rest: 39.1 ÷ 46 × 100 = 85.0 kg urea per acre. MOP (0-0-60) for potash: 16.2 ÷ 60 × 100 = 27.0 kg MOP per acre. In 50 kg bags: roughly 1 bag of DAP, 1.7 bags of urea and half a bag of MOP per acre. For the full derivation of this formula, see our guide on the NPK fertilizer calculation formula.",
