      "Their second strength is the slow, steady release of nutrients, including secondary nutrients and micronutrients that straight chemical fertilizers often lack. Manure supplies small amounts of zinc, iron, and boron alongside the big three nutrients. These extras rarely make the label, but over years they help prevent the hidden micronutrient deficiencies that quietly cap yields.",
      "The third strength is cost and availability for livestock-owning households. If you already keep cattle, manure is essentially free apart from labor. Composting crop residue costs nothing but time and turns a waste problem into a resource.",
